-- §6.2 T3/T4 — THE CLOSED HALF of the #1155 (F-3c) closedness gate. Pairs with
-- s6-2-t4-open-goal-deferred.mdk, which is this file with ONE TOKEN changed.
--
-- Two ⊑-incomparable user impls (`Q Int y z` vs `Q x Bool z`). Since F-3d
-- (#614/#311) coherence condition (a) only WARNS about that pair
-- (`W-INCOMPARABLE-IMPLS` at the second declaration), so the goal-site reject below
-- is the whole of this file's verdict -- and its sibling, whose goal is not closed,
-- ACCEPTS. Before F-3d (a) rejected at the declaration too; that did not end the
-- compile either, because errors ACCUMULATE (AGENTS.md: "phases push into
-- diagnostics.mdk rather than raising on the first error"), so both impls were
-- registered in KeyBuckets and the goal reached the min⊑ selector regardless.
--
-- SPEC ANSWER (hand-derived from §3 `match`, §3 ⊑, §6 C1 and §6.2 T3):
--   The goal is `Sh (Q Int Bool (List Int))`. Every argument is GROUND, so it is
--   closed under T3 and C1 applies. Both impls match it (`Q Int y z` at
--   y↦Bool, z↦List Int; `Q x Bool z` at x↦Int, z↦List Int) and neither ⊑ the
--   other (`Int` cannot be an instance of `x`... in the direction that matters:
--   no substitution on either head's variables produces the other, since each
--   pins a DIFFERENT argument to a constructor). No ⊑-minimum ⇒ ambiguous
--   overlap ⇒ the goal-site reject fires, ALONGSIDE the declaration-time warning.
--
-- So this file carries `T-AMBIGUOUS-INSTANCE` and its sibling must not. That
-- DIFFERENCE was the whole assertion until F-3d, when the pair also began to
-- differ on VERDICT (this REJECTs, the sibling ACCEPTs). The code assertion stays:
-- it is what says the reject came from the min⊑ arm rather than from anything else.
--
-- ⚠️ THIS PAIR REFUTES A CLAIM F-3C ORIGINALLY SHIPPED. Its first version argued
-- the closedness gate was UNREACHABLE, on the grounds that a ⊑-incomparable user
-- pair with a shared bare-variable argument always has unifying heads and so is
-- "rejected at the declaration". The premise is true and the conclusion does not
-- follow: a declaration-time rejection is not an early exit. The gate is
-- reachable; what was true is only that it had no USER-VISIBLE effect while every
-- program reaching it was already rejected by coherence (a). F-3d demoted (a), and
-- this pair now discriminates an ACCEPT from a REJECT.
data Q a b c = Q a b c

interface Sh a where
  sh : a -> Int

impl Sh (Q Int y z) where
  sh _ = 1

impl Sh (Q x Bool z) where
  sh _ = 2

main = println (sh (Q 1 True [1]))
